<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Livonia</h1><p class="st-meta">1561 CE – 1621 CE · capital Riga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Polish-Lithuanian Livonia, created when the crusader state dissolved in 1561, and fought over by Sweden and Poland for six decades.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vilnius-1561">Treaty of Vilnius</a> in 1561, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/archbishopric-of-riga">Archbishopric of Riga</a> submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a>. The lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> became the Duchy of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-confederation">Livonia</a>, with the last master, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-kettler">Gotthard Kettler</a>, as administrator. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569 it was held jointly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> and later conflicts devastated the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-bathory">Stephen Báthory</a> drove out the Russians by 1582 and reorganised <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-confederation">Livonia</a> into districts, promoting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> at Riga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a>, which offended the Lutheran German nobility and towns. Riga, a free city until 1581, was the chief port.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> fought over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-confederation">Livonia</a>; the Polish victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kircholm">Kircholm</a> in 1605 delayed Swedish conquest, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustav II Adolf</a> took Riga in 1621. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-altmark">Truce of Altmark</a> in 1629 left most of the duchy as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/swedish-livonia">Swedish Livonia</a>, while the south-east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a>, remained Polish as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflanty-voivodeship">Inflanty Voivodeship</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-livonia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Livonia" width="320" height="252"><figcaption>Image: Der Eberswalder, some modification by Lexicon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polish-Lithuanian_Commonwealth_(1619).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1561 CE – 1621 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Riga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sigismund II Augustus, Gotthard Kettler, Stephen Báthory</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran and Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Latvian, Polish, Estonian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Latvia, Estonia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Inflanty, Polish Livonia, Duchy of Livonia (1561-1621)</dd></dl></aside></div>
